      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5fbbbabb6998b157f8820d50|full_name}: A subscription base just for the few people that want early beta features I think is alright, most won't do it but for the few who really are into it then it is cool. But for the rest like icons and whatever like you are talking about it should just be a one time payment, that is a WAYYY better alternative and then u don't just lose your stuff once it's done.\nAnd also if anyone wants to compare VRC to other subscription websites, just because other businesses do it doesn't make it right.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2020-11-23T14:01:57.931Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Oh boy, here we go. This game deserves to make money, we all know that. This however is not the way to do it. A subscription based monetization system feels very scummy to me. If you take into account that you lose the features as soon as you stop paying (no matter how long you have been subscribed) then you start to see the issue. There is possibly a way to make a subscription model work (maybe put access to the beta sdk's and such behind it) but this as it sits currently is not a proper solution to monetization. I am going to give the vrchat team the benefit of the doubt and say they will only lock insignificant features behind a paywall HOWEVER, no matter the significance of these features losing them as soon as you end your subscription is absurd. (Keep in mind you could have payed hundreds of dollars and then immediately lost those features until you pay again.) \n\n       On another note, there is already a class structure that exists within the community. There are an amount of trusted users (not all obviously) who think that they are 'holier than thou' and will shun other users for not playing as long or uploading as much content as they have. This also works in reverse. Because of that attitude many people don't see the value in the trust system at all. Adding an icon that means \"hey I supported the devs!\" would be great HOWEVER when you take the trust system into account and how people have already treated that system, an issue arises with how those players will treat non supporters and vise versa. I feel this way because currently a substantial amount of players are dissatisfied with the state of the game and will take their aggression out people who support the developers. \n\n       Now, I am definitely not the person to ask for options when it comes to monetizing the game. I would honestly be happy paying a one time fee of 60 dollars just like any other game. I think that to gain access to features it should be a one time payment and things that really don't matter to the average player (ie, beta access or something similar) could be behind a subscription. With all that said I'm going to cut myself off here. I do want to support this game and I did not intend to come off as rude, I just don't see this as a good way to support the game. \n\n       TLDR: Subscriptions bad, One time payments good.",
